n the same grade of priesthood\nL56: \nL57: 【19†Image†prod.cdn-mw.com】\nL58: ## Did you know?\nL59: \nL60: Quorum Has a Legal History\nL61: It takes two drama queens to 【20†tango】, three Nervous Nellies to change a lightbulb, and 218 U.S. 【21†House Representatives†www.britannica.com】 to constitute a formal meeting. Each of these minimums—especially the last one—may be described as a quorum. This word, which can be pluralized as quorums or quora, comes directly from the Latin word quorum, which translates as “of whom.” At one time, this Latin quorum was used in the wording of the commissions granting power to justices of the peace in England.\nL62: Later, when it became an English noun, quorum initially referred to the number of justices of the peace who had to be present in order to hear a case. That sense is now rare, and today quorum is used to refer to the minimum number of people required to be present at a meeting in order for official business to take place. It can also be used more broadly to mean simply “a select group.”\nL63: ## Examples of quorum in a Sentence\nL64: \nL65: We need five people to make a quorum.\nL66: \nL67: Recent Examples on the Web\nL68: \nL69: Examples are automatically compiled from online sources to show current usage. 【14†Read More】 Opinions expressed in the examples do not represent those of Merriam-Webster or its editors. 【22†Send us feedback†docs.google.com】.\nL70: By leaving the state, Democrats broke quorum and delayed a vote. — CBS News, 28 Oct. 2026 Investors and lawmakers must demand that the empty Commission seats be filled, that the definition of a quorum of one be reversed, and that information previously provided to the market be restored before capital is driven from American capitalism. — Andrew Behar, Fortune, 26 Oct. 2026 Two other board members subsequently walked out, leaving the board without a quorum and forcing the meeting to adjourn.\nL71: — Charlotte Observer, 20 Oct. 2026 The Supreme Court was unable to consider a petition to review a copyright case after a rare lack of quorum due to four justices recusing themselves from evaluating the petition. — Jack Birle, The Washington Examiner, 19 Oct. 2026 【23†See All Example Sentences for quorum】\nL72: ## Word History\nL73: \nL74: Etymology\nL75: \nL76: Middle English, quorum of justices of the peace, from Latin, of whom, genitive plural of qui who; from the wording of the commission formerly issued to justices of the peace\nL77: \nL78: First Known Use\nL79: \nL80: 1596, in the meaning defined at 【24†sense 2】\nL81: \nL82: Time Traveler\nL83: \nL84: The first known use of quorum was in 1596\nL85: \nL86: 【25†See more words from the same year】\nL87: \nL88: ## Browse Nearby Words\nL89: \nL90: 【26†Quoratean】\nL91: \nL92: quorum\nL93: \nL94: 【27†quorum call】\nL95: \nL96: 【28†See all Nearby Words】\nL97: ## Articles Related to quorum\nL98: \nL99: 【29†The Words of the Week - Aug. 8 Dictionary lookups from Texas, Major League Baseball, and bats】 【30†8 Latin Phrases That Mean Something... Same Latin, different meaning】\nL100: \nL101: ## Podcast\nL102: \nL103: ## Cite this Entry\nL104: \nL105: Style\nL106: \nL107: [Select]\nL108: \nL109: “Quorum.” Merriam-Webster.com Dictionary, Merriam-Webster, https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/quorum. Accessed 2 Nov. 2026.\nL110: \nL111: 【31†Image†prod.cdn-mw.com】Copy Citation\nL112: ## Kids Definition\nL113: \nL114: quorum\nL115: \nL116: noun\nL117: \nL118: quo·​rum 【16†ˈkwōr-əm 】\nL119: \nL120: ˈkwȯr-\nL121: \nL122: : the number of members of an organization required to be present in order for business to be carried on\nL123: ## Legal Definition\nL124: \nL125: quorum\nL126: \nL127: noun\nL128: \nL129: quo·​rum 【16†ˈkwōr-əm 】\nL130: \nL131: : the number (as a majority) of members or officers that must be present to conduct business\nL132: \nL133: lacked a quorum at the meeting of shareholders\nL134: \nL135: Etymology\nL136: \nL137: Middle English, a select number of English justices of the peace 